   "source": [
    "## Step 4 – Why should k be odd? (item 3)\n",
    "\n",
    "With **two** classes, an odd $$k$$ always gives a clear majority. With an even $$k$$ (for example $$k = 4$$ with 2 neighbours of each class) a tie is possible, and the result would depend on the order of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset in Figure 3 of the sheet has two classes, so this argument applies directly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Note:** Iris has **three** classes. Even for odd $$k$$ a tie is possible (for example one neighbour of each class for $$k = 3$$). In the code, ties are resolved by the order of the training data."
   ]
  }
